WEBVTT

NOTE From evidence to forecast

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.889
A probability in a forecast is not a fact about the world.

2
00:00:03.889 --> 00:00:05.767
It is a summary of evidence.

3
00:00:06.267 --> 00:00:09.084
The word probability covers three ideas.

4
00:00:09.084 --> 00:00:14.789
It can mean how strongly evidence supports a claim, or how confident a person is.

5
00:00:14.789 --> 00:00:18.100
Or a physical tendency, whatever anyone thinks.

6
00:00:18.567 --> 00:00:22.728
Counting cases needs a reference class, the group you count over.

7
00:00:22.728 --> 00:00:27.915
One philosopher notes that he is a man, a non-smoker, and a philosophy professor.

8
00:00:27.915 --> 00:00:31.500
Each group gives a different chance of living to eighty.

9
00:00:31.967 --> 00:00:37.404
In 1814 Pierre-Simon Laplace took recorded history as five thousand years of

10
00:00:37.404 --> 00:00:43.200
sunrises. His rule put the odds of one more sunrise at nearly two million to one.

11
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:48.618
He added that anyone who knows what drives the seasons would put the odds far

12
00:00:48.618 --> 00:00:52.267
higher. Same sunrise, more evidence, a different number.

13
00:00:52.733 --> 00:00:55.318
Ignorance does not fix a number either.

14
00:00:55.318 --> 00:00:58.498
A factory makes cubes with sides up to one foot.

15
00:00:58.498 --> 00:01:03.932
Spread belief evenly over side length, and a side under half a foot gets one half.

16
00:01:03.932 --> 00:01:09.167
Over face area, one quarter. Over volume, one eighth. One event, three answers.

17
00:01:09.667 --> 00:01:13.534
Evidence should move a number, and Bayes' rule says how far.

18
00:01:13.534 --> 00:01:18.626
It starts from the base rate, how common something is before any test, and then

19
00:01:18.626 --> 00:01:20.367
weighs what the test shows.

20
00:01:20.833 --> 00:01:25.127
A 1995 study by Gigerenzer and Hoffrage used a screening test.

21
00:01:25.127 --> 00:01:29.767
Take one thousand women aged forty. Ten of them have breast cancer.

22
00:01:30.233 --> 00:01:32.236
Eight of those ten test positive.

23
00:01:32.236 --> 00:01:37.333
Of the nine hundred and ninety women without cancer, ninety-five also test positive.

24
00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:42.707
So one hundred and three women test positive, and only eight of them have cancer.

25
00:01:42.707 --> 00:01:44.767
That is seven point eight percent.

26
00:01:45.267 --> 00:01:50.118
In this reference class the disease is rare, so most positive tests come from

27
00:01:50.118 --> 00:01:51.000
healthy women.

28
00:01:51.467 --> 00:01:55.286
Given as percentages, this problem defeats experts.

29
00:01:55.286 --> 00:02:01.501
In a 1982 study by Eddy, ninety-five of a hundred physicians said seventy to eighty

30
00:02:01.501 --> 00:02:02.100
percent.

31
00:02:02.567 --> 00:02:03.598
Counting helps.

32
00:02:03.598 --> 00:02:09.098
In the 1995 study, correct answers rose from sixteen percent with percentages to

33
00:02:09.098 --> 00:02:11.367
about half with counts of people.

34
00:02:11.867 --> 00:02:14.559
One number can hide how much you know.

35
00:02:14.559 --> 00:02:19.307
A coin that landed heads in half of a hundred tosses gets one half.

36
00:02:19.307 --> 00:02:24.833
So may a coin you have never seen. Its honest summary is a range, zero to one.

37
00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:29.201
Daniel Ellsberg's urn, from 1961, makes the same point.

38
00:02:29.201 --> 00:02:34.733
It holds thirty red balls, and sixty black and yellow balls in an unknown mix.

39
00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.460
The chance of red is one third.

40
00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:41.542
The chance of black is anywhere from zero to two thirds.

41
00:02:41.542 --> 00:02:46.207
People often bet on this urn in a way no single number explains.

42
00:02:46.207 --> 00:02:48.467
A range offers one explanation.

43
00:02:48.933 --> 00:02:51.258
Witness evidence has another shape.

44
00:02:51.258 --> 00:02:56.108
In Glenn Shafer's example, you trust your friend Betty nine times in ten.

45
00:02:56.108 --> 00:02:58.300
She says a limb fell on your car.

46
00:02:58.767 --> 00:03:04.195
Her word supports a belief of zero point nine that it fell, and a belief of zero

47
00:03:04.195 --> 00:03:08.267
that it did not. That zero means no evidence, not certainty.

48
00:03:08.733 --> 00:03:13.996
Dempster-Shafer theory gives each claim two numbers: a belief and an upper limit.

49
00:03:13.996 --> 00:03:19.194
If a second friend, equally reliable, independently agrees, belief rises to zero

50
00:03:19.194 --> 00:03:20.233
point nine nine.

51
00:03:20.733 --> 00:03:23.286
All these numbers lean on the past.

52
00:03:23.286 --> 00:03:29.195
In 1748 David Hume wrote that every conclusion from experience assumes the future

53
00:03:29.195 --> 00:03:30.800
will be like the past.

54
00:03:31.267 --> 00:03:33.381
That is the problem of induction.

55
00:03:33.381 --> 00:03:38.633
The sun failing to rise tomorrow is no contradiction, and the assumption cannot be

56
00:03:38.633 --> 00:03:41.900
proved from experience without arguing in a circle.

57
00:03:42.367 --> 00:03:45.441
Picking the best explanation adds a further gap.

58
00:03:45.441 --> 00:03:50.821
The best of the ones we thought of can be what Bas van Fraassen called the best of a

59
00:03:50.821 --> 00:03:51.333
bad lot.

60
00:03:51.833 --> 00:03:53.944
Weather adds a harder problem.

61
00:03:53.944 --> 00:03:59.572
In 1963 Edward Lorenz showed that in a simple model of rising warm air, slightly

62
00:03:59.572 --> 00:04:03.933
different starting states can grow into very different states.

63
00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:07.179
So forecasters run the model many times.

64
00:04:07.179 --> 00:04:12.528
The European Centre for Medium-Range Weather Forecasts starts fifty runs from

65
00:04:12.528 --> 00:04:17.600
slightly altered states, plus one unaltered run. That set is an ensemble.

66
00:04:18.067 --> 00:04:24.725
In one forecast from 26 May 2017, the runs sat close together thirty hours ahead.

67
00:04:24.725 --> 00:04:27.767
Six days ahead they had spread apart.

68
00:04:28.233 --> 00:04:29.870
The spread is information.

69
00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:34.778
The centre calls the probability of an event the most consistent way to convey

70
00:04:34.778 --> 00:04:36.100
forecast uncertainty.

71
00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:39.404
Not every claim about the future is a forecast.

72
00:04:39.404 --> 00:04:44.237
A forecast starts from the present state and says what will happen, in a form you

73
00:04:44.237 --> 00:04:44.833
can check.

74
00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:49.916
A projection says what would happen if an assumption holds, such as a path of

75
00:04:49.916 --> 00:04:53.033
emissions. That assumption may or may not come true.

76
00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:58.831
A scenario is a plausible story about the future, with no probability attached.

77
00:04:58.831 --> 00:05:03.892
The United Nations climate panel says scenarios are neither predictions nor

78
00:05:03.892 --> 00:05:04.567
forecasts.

79
00:05:05.033 --> 00:05:10.163
A counterfactual asks what would have happened had the past been different.

80
00:05:10.163 --> 00:05:15.907
As Donald Rubin noted in 1974, we see a person's outcome with a treatment or without

81
00:05:15.907 --> 00:05:16.933
it, never both.

82
00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:23.011
Judea Pearl puts these questions on three rungs: seeing, doing, and imagining.

83
00:05:23.011 --> 00:05:28.333
Data from a lower rung cannot, alone, answer a question from a higher one.

84
00:05:28.833 --> 00:05:32.070
A forecast earns trust only when it is scored.

85
00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:37.841
The Brier score, from 1950, measures the squared gap between forecast and outcome.

86
00:05:37.841 --> 00:05:38.967
Lower is better.

87
00:05:39.433 --> 00:05:44.773
In one tournament's form of the score, saying ninety percent costs zero point zero

88
00:05:44.773 --> 00:05:49.267
two if right, and one point six two if wrong. The worst score is two.

89
00:05:49.733 --> 00:05:54.192
A forecaster is calibrated when the events they call seventy percent happen about

90
00:05:54.192 --> 00:05:55.733
seventy percent of the time.

91
00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:57.650
People often are not.

92
00:05:57.650 --> 00:06:03.241
In studies reviewed by Tversky and Kahneman in 1974, ranges given as ninety-eight

93
00:06:03.241 --> 00:06:06.900
percent sure missed about thirty percent of the time.

94
00:06:07.367 --> 00:06:09.104
Forecasting can be trained.

95
00:06:09.104 --> 00:06:14.187
The Good Judgment Project found three things that helped: probability training,

96
00:06:14.187 --> 00:06:17.533
teamwork, and grouping the top two percent together.

97
00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:20.716
Even they were overconfident at the edge.

98
00:06:20.716 --> 00:06:26.149
Early forecasts of one hundred percent came true only about seventy percent of the

99
00:06:26.149 --> 00:06:28.733
time. Late in a question, about ninety.

100
00:06:29.233 --> 00:06:33.464
A scored number still fails if the listener hears a different one.

101
00:06:33.464 --> 00:06:35.067
That is the next problem.
